      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2017 John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "The crosstalk between chondrocytes and adipose-derived stem cells (ADSCs) could regulate the secretion of multiple growth factors. However, it is not clear how the paracrine action in co-culture systems affect cell migration. This study focused on the changes of cell migration of ADSCs and chondrocytes in co-culture conditions."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "Primary ADSCs and chondrocytes were isolated from Sprague-Dawley rat. Transwell co-culture systems, inoculated with ADSCs and chondrocytes, were established in vitro. The morphology of the cells was observed 7 days post-seeding by inverted phase-contrast microscope. Additionally, the cytoskeleton changes were investigated by immunofluorescence staining. To detect the abundance of Vinculin, we used immunofluorescence and Western blotting. Additionally, the expression level of MMP-2, Hey1 and Hes1 was examined to determine the mechanisms of co-culture-induced cell migration changes."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The migration of ADSCs and chondrocytes in co-culture conditions significantly decreased compared with that in mono-culture groups, accompanied by the decrease of filopodia and the expression level of MMP-2."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The overall study showed that the migration of ADSCs and chondrocytes differs significantly depending on culture conditions. Moreover, the Notch signalling pathway may be involved in this process. Accordingly, by studying changes in migration caused by co-culture, we obtained new insight into the crosstalk between ADSCs and chondrocytes."
          }
        ]
      },
